body.proof_tex:
Bounded outcomes imply \(\operatorname{Var}(Y(a))\leq1\). Thus the stated feasibility conditions are necessary; they are sufficient for nonemptiness by taking prompt symmetric Rademacher outcomes and the deterministic admissible policy. On this nonempty regular class, the variance comparison (9), the regular-variance assumption, and the deterministic quota orders give constants \(0<c_0<C_0<\infty\) such that
\[
 c_0T^{-2q}\leq s_{T,\Delta}^2\leq C_0T^{-2q}. \tag{27}
\]
The studentized theorem and the old-cohort correction lemma give
\[
 \frac{\widehat\tau_{T,\Delta}-\tau}{\widehat s_{T,\Delta}}
 =\frac{\widehat\tau_{T,\Delta}-\tau+b_{T,\Delta}}
 {\widehat s_{T,\Delta}}
 -\frac{b_{T,\Delta}}{\widehat s_{T,\Delta}},
\]
and, by \(|b_{T,\Delta}|\leq B_{T,\Delta}\), (17), and (27),
\[
 \sup_{(P,\Pi,T)\in\mathfrak M_{T,\Delta}^{\mathrm{reg}}(\kappa)}
 \frac{|b_{T,\Delta}|}{s_{T,\Delta}}
 \leq C T^q(1+T+\Delta_T)^{-\beta}. \tag{28}
\]
If the right side tends to zero, variance consistency makes the bias term uniformly \(o_P(1)\), while the first term is uniformly standard normal. The ordinary \(z_{1-\eta/2}\) interval therefore has coverage \(1-\eta+o(1)\) uniformly.

For necessity, use the attaining construction from the boundary theorem with its hidden delay replaced by \(H_T+1\) and
\[
 \delta_T=L(1+H_T)^{-\beta}
\]
eventually. Namely, hidden signs are opposite across arms, and the prompt Rademacher means are chosen to cancel each arm's hidden mean. Consequently each eventual arm outcome has mean zero and variance one, so the construction belongs to every support-feasible regular class. Its hidden atoms are not observed by \(H_T\); also \(g_{T,\Delta}<h_{T,\Delta}<H_T+1\), so \(U_{a,\Delta}=0\). The visible variance tends to one and
\[
 \frac{b_{T,\Delta}}{s_{T,\Delta}}
 \asymp T^q(1+H_T)^{-\beta}. \tag{29}
\]
If the product in (29) converges to a finite \(u_0>0\), its ratio has a further positive finite limit \(u\). The centered triangular-array statistic converges to \(N(0,1)\), so the ordinary-Wald coverage along that subsequence converges to
\[
 \Phi(z_{1-\eta/2}-u)+\Phi(z_{1-\eta/2}+u)-1<1-\eta. \tag{30}
\]
If the product diverges, the centered statistic remains tight while \(b_{T,\Delta}/\widehat s_{T,\Delta}\to\infty\), and coverage tends to zero. If the product merely fails to converge to zero, there is a subsequence on which it is bounded away from zero; a further subsequence falls into one of the two preceding cases. Hence uniform asymptotic honesty fails unless the product tends to zero. This proves the equivalence. For \(\Delta_T=0\), the product is \(T^{q-\beta}(1+o(1))\), which tends to zero exactly when \(\beta>q\).
